# Chemistry: physical chemistry. Some basic concepts, atomic structure,
# chemical bonding, chemical thermodynamics, solutions, equilibrium, redox
# and electrochemistry, chemical kinetics (JEE (Main) 2027 syllabus units 1
# to 8). Key written first; numerical value answers are computed here and
# asserted by N().
from math import log, log2

ITEMS = [
    # ---------- Practice Test 1: Section A ----------
    S(1, 1, "Mole concept", "How many moles of oxygen atoms are there in 4.9 g of sulphuric acid ($M = 98$ g/mol)?",
      ["0.2 mol", "0.05 mol", "0.1 mol", "0.15 mol"],
      "4.9 g is $\\frac{4.9}{98} = 0.05$ mol of H$_2$SO$_4$, and each molecule has 4 O atoms, so there are $4 \\times 0.05 = 0.2$ mol of O atoms. 0.05 mol counts molecules, not atoms."),
    S(1, 1, "Quantum numbers", "What is the largest number of electrons that can have $n = 3$ and $l = 2$?",
      ["10", "2", "5", "6"],
      "$l = 2$ is a d subshell with $2l + 1 = 5$ orbitals, each holding 2 electrons: 10 in all. 5 counts orbitals, and 6 is the capacity of a p subshell."),
    S(1, 2, "VSEPR theory", "What is the shape of the BrF$_5$ molecule?",
      ["Square pyramidal", "Trigonal bipyramidal", "Square planar", "Octahedral"],
      "Br has 5 bond pairs and 1 lone pair, an octahedral arrangement of 6 pairs. With one corner taken by the lone pair, the five F atoms form a square pyramid. Trigonal bipyramidal would need 5 pairs and no lone pair."),
    S(1, 2, "Gibbs energy", "A reaction has $\\Delta H = +40$ kJ/mol and $\\Delta S = +125$ J/(K mol). When is it spontaneous?",
      ["Above 320 K", "Below 320 K", "At every temperature", "Above 0.32 K"],
      "$\\Delta G = \\Delta H - T\\Delta S < 0$ needs $T > \\frac{\\Delta H}{\\Delta S} = \\frac{40\\,000}{125} = 320$ K. Mixing kJ with J gives the 0.32 K slip."),
    S(1, 2, "pH", "What is the pH of 0.0005 M barium hydroxide solution at 25 °C, assuming complete ionisation?",
      ["11", "3", "10.7", "12"],
      "Each Ba(OH)$_2$ gives two OH$^-$ ions, so $[\\text{OH}^-] = 0.001$ M, pOH $= 3$ and pH $= 14 - 3 = 11$. 10.7 takes $[\\text{OH}^-]$ as 0.0005 M, forgetting the factor 2, and 3 is the pOH."),
    S(1, 2, "Electrochemical cells", "$E^\\circ(\\text{Ag}^+/\\text{Ag}) = +0.80$ V and $E^\\circ(\\text{Ni}^{2+}/\\text{Ni}) = -0.25$ V. What is $E^\\circ$ for the cell $\\text{Ni} \\mid \\text{Ni}^{2+} \\parallel \\text{Ag}^+ \\mid \\text{Ag}$?",
      ["1.05 V", "-1.05 V", "0.55 V", "1.85 V"],
      "$E^\\circ_{cell} = E^\\circ_{cathode} - E^\\circ_{anode} = 0.80 - (-0.25) = 1.05$ V. Standard potentials are not multiplied by the 2 in the balanced equation, so 1.85 V is wrong."),
    # ---------- Practice Test 1: Section B ----------
    N(1, 2, "Chemical kinetics", "In a first-order reaction the concentration of the reactant falls from 0.8 M to 0.1 M in 45 minutes. What is the half-life, in minutes?",
      round(45 / log2(0.8 / 0.1)), 15,
      "0.8 to 0.4 to 0.2 to 0.1 M is 3 halvings, and for a first-order reaction each takes the same time. So $t_{1/2} = \\frac{45}{3} = 15$ min."),
    N(1, 1, "Molarity", "What mass of glucose ($M = 180$ g/mol), in grams, is needed to make 500 mL of a 0.2 M solution?",
      round(0.2 * 0.5 * 180), 18,
      "Moles needed $= 0.2 \\times 0.5 = 0.1$ mol, and $0.1 \\times 180 = 18$ g."),
    N(1, 2, "Electrolysis", "A current of 2 A passes through copper(II) sulphate solution for 4825 s. How many millimoles of copper are deposited? (Take $F = 96\\,500$ C/mol.)",
      round(2 * 4825 / 96500 / 2 * 1000), 50,
      "$Q = 2 \\times 4825 = 9650$ C $= 0.1$ F. Cu$^{2+}$ needs 2 electrons per atom, so $0.05$ mol $= 50$ mmol of copper is deposited."),
    # ---------- Practice Test 2: Section A ----------
    S(2, 1, "Quantum numbers", "Which set of quantum numbers is not allowed for an electron?",
      ["$n = 2,\\ l = 2,\\ m = 0$", "$n = 3,\\ l = 2,\\ m = -2$", "$n = 2,\\ l = 1,\\ m = 1$", "$n = 4,\\ l = 0,\\ m = 0$"],
      "$l$ runs from 0 to $n - 1$, so $n = 2$ allows only $l = 0$ or 1. The other sets obey $0 \\leq l \\leq n - 1$ and $-l \\leq m \\leq l$."),
    S(2, 2, "Molecular orbital theory", "Which of these species is diamagnetic?",
      ["O₂²⁻", "O₂", "O₂⁻", "O₂⁺"],
      "O$_2$ has two unpaired electrons in its $\\pi^*$ orbitals. O$_2^+$ and O$_2^-$ each have one. O$_2^{2-}$ fills both $\\pi^*$ orbitals, so all its electrons are paired."),
    S(2, 1, "Entropy", "For which change is the entropy change of the system negative?",
      ["Water freezing to ice", "Ice melting to water", "A gas expanding into a vacuum", "Water evaporating"],
      "Freezing locks molecules into an ordered lattice, so the system's entropy falls. Melting, evaporating and free expansion all spread energy and matter more widely, raising entropy."),
    S(2, 2, "Equilibrium constants", "For $\\text{N}_2(g) + 3\\text{H}_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2\\text{NH}_3(g)$, how is $K_p$ related to $K_c$?",
      ["$K_p = K_c(RT)^{-2}$", "$K_p = K_c(RT)^{2}$", "$K_p = K_c$", "$K_p = K_c(RT)^{-1}$"],
      "$K_p = K_c(RT)^{\\Delta n_g}$ with $\\Delta n_g = 2 - 4 = -2$. Taking reactants minus products gives the wrong sign, $+2$."),
    S(2, 2, "Rate law", "The rate law is $r = k[A][B]^2$. If $[A]$ is tripled and $[B]$ is halved, by what factor does the rate change?",
      ["3/4", "3/2", "3", "6"],
      "The new rate is $k(3[A])\\left(\\frac{[B]}{2}\\right)^2 = 3 \\times \\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{3}{4}$ of the old one. 3/2 forgets to square the factor $\\frac{1}{2}$ for $[B]$."),
    S(2, 2, "Colligative properties", "Which 0.01 molal aqueous solution has the lowest freezing point, assuming complete dissociation?",
      ["Al₂(SO₄)₃", "K₂SO₄", "NaCl", "Sucrose"],
      "Depression of freezing point is $iK_fm$. Al$_2$(SO$_4$)$_3$ gives 5 ions ($i = 5$), K$_2$SO$_4$ 3, NaCl 2 and sucrose 1, so the aluminium sulphate solution freezes lowest."),
    # ---------- Practice Test 2: Section B ----------
    N(2, 1, "Stoichiometry", "What mass of oxygen, in grams, is needed to burn 8 g of methane completely?",
      round(8 / 16 * 2 * 32), 32,
      "CH$_4$ + 2O$_2$ $\\to$ CO$_2$ + 2H$_2$O. 8 g of methane is 0.5 mol, which needs 1 mol, or 32 g, of O$_2$."),
    N(2, 2, "Buffer solutions", "A buffer contains a weak acid of $\\text{p}K_a = 5$ and its sodium salt, with the salt concentration 100 times the acid concentration. What is its pH?",
      round(5 + log(100, 10)), 7,
      "By the Henderson equation, pH $= \\text{p}K_a + \\log\\frac{[\\text{salt}]}{[\\text{acid}]} = 5 + \\log 100 = 5 + 2 = 7$."),
    N(2, 2, "Chemical kinetics", "A first-order reaction has a half-life of 12 minutes. How many minutes does it take to be 93.75% complete?",
      round(12 * log2(1 / 0.0625)), 48,
      "93.75% complete leaves $\\frac{1}{16} = \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^4$, which takes 4 half-lives: $4 \\times 12 = 48$ min."),
    # ---------- Drill only ----------
    S(0, 2, "Real gases", "At very high pressure the compressibility factor $Z$ of a real gas is greater than 1. What is the main cause?",
      ["The volume of the molecules", "Attraction between molecules", "Collisions with the walls", "Ionisation of the gas"],
      "At high pressure the molecules' own volume (van der Waals $b$) is a large part of the container, so the gas is harder to compress than an ideal gas and $Z > 1$. Attraction ($a$) lowers $Z$ below 1, which matters more at moderate pressure."),
    S(0, 1, "Thermodynamic properties", "Which of these is an intensive property?",
      ["Temperature", "Volume", "Enthalpy", "Mass"],
      "An intensive property does not depend on the amount of substance. Splitting a sample in two halves its volume, mass and enthalpy but leaves its temperature unchanged."),
    S(0, 2, "Conductance", "Statement I: The molar conductivity of a weak electrolyte rises steeply on dilution. Statement II: The degree of dissociation of a weak electrolyte rises on dilution. Which is correct?",
      [STATEMENTS[0], STATEMENTS[1], STATEMENTS[2], STATEMENTS[3]],
      "By Ostwald's dilution law, $\\alpha \\approx \\sqrt{\\frac{K_a}{c}}$ grows as $c$ falls, so many more ions form and $\\Lambda_m$ rises steeply. Both statements are true."),
    S(0, 2, "Oxidation number", "What is the oxidation number of sulphur in peroxodisulphuric acid, H$_2$S$_2$O$_8$?",
      ["+6", "+5", "+7", "+8"],
      "H$_2$S$_2$O$_8$ has one peroxide O-O link, whose two oxygens are $-1$ each; the other six are $-2$. So $2(+1) + 2x + 6(-2) + 2(-1) = 0$ gives $x = +6$. Taking all eight oxygens as $-2$ gives the impossible +7."),
    N(0, 1, "Molarity", "How many millimoles of NaOH are there in 200 mL of a 0.5 M solution?",
      round(200 * 0.5), 100,
      "Millimoles $=$ molarity $\\times$ volume in mL $= 0.5 \\times 200 = 100$."),
    N(0, 2, "Order of reaction", "Doubling the concentration of the only reactant makes the rate 8 times faster. What is the order of the reaction?",
      round(log2(8)), 3,
      "Rate $\\propto [A]^n$, so $2^n = 8$ and $n = 3$."),
]
